==English==

===Etymology===
An [[allusion]] to the dairy cow, which once acquired may be milked on an ongoing basis.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-cash cow.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|business}} A [[product]], [[service]], or [[enterprise]] that generates ongoing, high net [[free cash flow]]s.
#: {{cot|en|star|question mark|dog}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1980|author=D. J. Hall|author2=M. A. Saias|title=Strategy Follows Structure!|work=Strategic Management Journal|volume=1|number=2|page=181|passage=The managers of a '''cash cow''' are primarily responsible for the generation of cash, and they should in principle be willing to give cash away to those other businesses in the portfolio which need it most.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Someone or something which is a [[dependable]] [[source]] of [[appreciable]] amounts of [[money]].
#: {{syn|en|moneymaker}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=11 May 2004|author=Dan Milmo|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/business/2004/may/11/pressandpublishing.thedailytelegraph|title=Press tycoon Lord Black used company as ‘'''cash cow'''’, says $1.25bn lawsuit|work=The Guardian|accessdate=30 Oct. 2008|passage=Hollinger International, the owner of the Daily and Sunday Telegraph, claimed in the 175-page filing that its former chief executive had treated the company as his "'''cash cow'''".}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date= 5 Oct 2004|url=http://www.theage.com.au/articles/2004/10/05/1096871856912.html|title=Britney's new digs|work=The Age (Australia)|accessdate=30 Oct. 2008|passage=Meanwhile, "Mr Spears is keen on protecting his '''cash cow''', um, wife. Brit's bodyguards have been teaching Kev how to shoot a handgun," the ''New York Post'' says.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021-01-30|author=Kellen Browning|title=They’re Flocking to America to Make a Fortune Playing Video Games|journal={{w|The New York Times}}|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/01/30/technology/esports-league-of-legends-america.html|passage=Many of the League Championship Series’ 10 teams are backed by billionaires who also own traditional U.S. sports teams. But the sport has not yet become a '''cash cow'''. To get in on League of Legends, teams had to pay Riot $10 million to $13 million.}}

